---
source: crates/wolf_mem/tests/mem_diagnostics.rs
expression: render_mem(src)
---
error[E1010]: `keep` may keep `d` in its `mut` argument `l`, and `l` outlives region `pass`, where `d`'s bytes live
  --> mem:///main.lu:13:29
   |
10 |         region pass {
   |                ---- region `pass` is created here
11 |             let d = Doc { title: "built {l.seq}", n: 1 }
   |                                  --------------- allocated here, into region `pass`
12 |             in st {
13 |                 keep(mut l, d)
   |                          - `l` is lent `mut` here: the callee may store any other argument's bytes in it
   |                             ^ this argument's bytes are in region `pass`, which is freed before `l` is
14 |             }
15 |         }
   |         - the region is freed here — everything in it is freed wholesale, as one unit
   |
   = note: a call may keep what it is handed: a callee can store one argument's bytes into a `mut`
     argument (`l.last = copy s` shares a `str`'s bytes, a `push` lends them), so a `mut`
     argument is taken to share the bytes of every other argument that can name bytes a
     region owns [mem.region.escape]. `in r { … }` aims what the callee ALLOCATES at `r`; an
     argument's bytes stay where they were built. Build the value where the `mut` argument
     lives: inside the `in` block (an interpolation there materializes in `r`), or outside
     the region block; or let the block's own value leave with `copy region { … }` and make
     the call after it [mem.region.copyout].
